  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nSurface boundary remains draped across the Ohio Valley region today \nwith periods of showers and storms continuing as numerous shortwaves \naloft move overhead through this evening. Showers and storms will be \ncapable of producing heavy rainfall with localized flooding \npossible. Both REFS and HREF composite reflectivity ensemble \npaintballs indicate two primary windows where shower and storm \ncoverage increase. The first window will be early this morning into \ndaybreak as a wave aloft moves overhead. Area of coverage will \ninclude portions of Northwest and Central Ohio. Another wave enters \nthe region late this morning/early afternoon where storms may dive \nsoutheast from Northern Indiana towards Central Ohio. Low confidence \nin any storm reaching severe limits today as best instability \nremains south and west of the forecast area. \n\nBy this evening, the front will exit to the south as Canadian high \npressure enters from the north. There may be some lingering showers \nthis evening before the high takes full control of the weather \npattern by Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nDry conditions are expected through Friday evening before high \npressure exits to the east Friday night into Saturday. Precipitation \nchances return from the northwest overnight Friday into Saturday as \nlow pressure lifts a warm front across the local area. The low will \nthen drag a cold front from northwest to southeast Saturday into \nSunday. The primary concern with this system continues to be heavy \nrainfall and localized flash flooding. WPC has maintained a Slight \nRisk (Level 2 of 4) Excessive Rainfall Outlook for this timeframe \nacross the western half of the forecast area. Highest axis of QPF \nremains north of the forecast area in a swath from Chicago/Milwaukee \neast towards Detroit.",
